WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:05.880
Crisis on Infinite Earths was a monumental
achievement, and a lot of fun, but it would've

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:09.700
been even better if a couple more DC characters
had showed up to play.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:14.919
In the end, we plain missed these folks, and
if you're a DC super-fan, you probably did

00:00:14.919 --> 00:00:15.919
too.

00:00:15.919 --> 00:00:19.380
Felicity Smoak wasn't supposed to be a major
player in the Arrowverse.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:24.520
Emily Bett Rickards' character was originally
conceived as a one-off guest appearance, designed

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:27.260
to pop up in a single episode and nothing
more.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:32.820
"Look, I don't wanna get in the middle of
some Shakespearean family drama thing."

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.300
"What?"

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.220
History had other plans.

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:41.840
Rickards' winning performance, as well as
her undeniable chemistry with Arrow star Stephen

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:46.420
Amell, quickly convinced Arrow's producers
to bring Felicity back for more.

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:50.570
It took less than a season for Felicity to
become part of Arrow's regular cast.

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:55.950
By season two, she was Arrow's heart and soul,
and ultimately ended up as both Oliver Queen's

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:58.310
wife and the mother of his children.

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:02.370
That's why it's so weird that Felicity sat
out Crisis entirely.

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:07.490
Sure, Rickards left Arrow after season seven,
but she's returning for the series finale.

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:11.030
She couldn't pop by for a quick cameo in the
big crossover event, too?

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:15.700
The Crisis wasn't just the biggest threat
the Arrowverse has ever faced, it was also

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:18.450
an epic sendoff for Oliver Queen.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:22.920
As such, it just felt strange that Felicity
wasn't there to say goodbye.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.659
In the comics, Crisis on Infinite Earths was
Wally West's time to shine.

00:01:27.659 --> 00:01:33.490
In Crisis on Infinite Earths #8, Barry Allen,
Wally's mentor, sacrificed himself to destroy

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:39.400
the Anti-Monitor's powerful antimatter gun,
saving the remaining universes from destruction.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.860
Once the Anti-Monitor's plans are foiled,
Wally West sheds the name Kid Flash and takes

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.360
over for Barry.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:46.640
Wally was more than a sidekick.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:50.590
He was the DC Universe's main Flash for almost
25 years.

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:56.450
Of course, on television, neither Barry nor
Flash star Grant Gustin are going anywhere.

00:01:56.450 --> 00:02:00.400
In the speedster ranks, Kid Flash still plays
second fiddle.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:05.409
Even so, it would've been nice to see Kid
Flash make an appearance in the CW's big crossover.

00:02:05.409 --> 00:02:10.420
Wally has been a regular fixture in the Arrowverse
since 2015, when actor Keiynan Lonsdale stepped

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:14.980
into the role, and while we haven't seen much
of Kid Flash since Legends of Tomorrow's season

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:19.800
three finale, Kid Flash is already scheduled
to return to The Flash after Crisis.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:24.050
Given everything that went down during the
event, Barry really could've used his help

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:25.050
here, too.

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:29.250
Of course, if you really want to see what
Wally and Felicity were up to during Crisis

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:33.611
on Infinite Earths, you can always check out
DC's spinoff comic, which was co-written by

00:02:33.611 --> 00:02:36.020
Crisis creator Marv Wolfman.

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:38.720
That's not the same as seeing the characters
on screen, though.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.760
Their absence stood out like a sore thumb.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.220
Crisis on Infinite Earths had a Batman.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.240
It had three different Supermen.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.310
Where the heck was Wonder Woman?

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:52.500
The third and final member of DC's trinity
isn't just one of the best-known superheroes

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:53.560
on the planet.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:58.630
The Amazonian warrior was the star of her
very own television show, which debuted in

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:01.680
1975 and starred actress and singer Lynda
Carter.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:05.270
In fact, Carter already has a role in the
Arrowverse.

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:10.491
In Supergirl, she plays Olivia Marsdin, a
shape-shifting alien who, at one point, was

00:03:10.491 --> 00:03:12.519
also the president of the United States.

00:03:12.519 --> 00:03:16.410
"Remind the world that there is still goodness
here.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:21.099
Be that beacon of hope that we still need."

00:03:21.099 --> 00:03:25.810
Carter appeared in five episodes of Supergirl,
and we're going to assume that executive producer

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:30.170
Greg Berlanti and the rest of the Crisis team
still have her phone number.

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:35.060
Maybe Warner Bros. didn't want Crisis on Infinite
Earths to steal any thunder from Wonder Woman

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:36.060
1984.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:40.280
Still, a cameo wouldn't have meant that Carter
needed to suit up.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:45.159
An older, retired Diana Prince would've fit
in well with Kevin Conroy's grizzled, jaded

00:03:45.159 --> 00:03:49.110
Bruce Wayne and Brandon Routh's greying Man
of Steel.

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:50.370
What a missed opportunity.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:56.370
The CW's mega-crossover featured characters
from almost every major television show based

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:58.000
on a DC comic.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.060
Birds of Prey?

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:00.460
Batman '66?

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:02.310
The '90s version of The Flash?

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:03.400
They're all there.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:08.580
Heck, even Lucifer, which is only tangentially
related to the DC Universe, got a brief but

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:09.750
memorable nod.

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:12.739
But the key word there is "almost."

00:04:12.739 --> 00:04:18.099
Lois and Clark: The New Adventures of Superman,
the hit '90s show that re-imagined the relationship

00:04:18.099 --> 00:04:24.189
between Lois Lane and Superman as a Moonlighting-style
romantic comedy, didn't get a single nod.

00:04:24.189 --> 00:04:25.330
No cameo.

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:28.599
No offhand mention or super-obscure Easter
egg.

00:04:28.599 --> 00:04:33.949
Heck, even Super Friends got a nod in Crisis
on Infinite Earths, but Lois and Clark, a

00:04:33.949 --> 00:04:36.169
legitimate ratings hit?

00:04:36.169 --> 00:04:37.319
Nothin'.

00:04:37.319 --> 00:04:42.639
Even stranger, both Lois and Clark's stars
have established relationships with the Arrowverse.

00:04:42.639 --> 00:04:46.960
Dean Cain, who played Superman on Lois and
Clark, has a recurring role on Supergirl as

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:49.690
Kara Danvers' adopted father.

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:55.180
Teri Hatcher played the Daxamite queen Rhea,
Supergirl's second season big bad.

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:58.300
Both actors are clearly open to appearing
on the CW.

00:04:58.300 --> 00:04:59.599
They've done it before.

00:04:59.599 --> 00:05:02.199
Just, oddly, not this time.

00:05:02.199 --> 00:05:06.819
The Joker plays a minor role in Crisis on
Infinite Earths, but you'd be forgiven for

00:05:06.819 --> 00:05:07.979
not noticing.

00:05:07.979 --> 00:05:09.639
He doesn't appear on screen.

00:05:09.639 --> 00:05:11.330
Nobody ever says his name.

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:12.860
Still, he's there.

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:18.199
When our heroes visit Earth-96 to recruit
Brandon Routh's Man of Tomorrow, the alternate-universe

00:05:18.199 --> 00:05:24.509
Superman mentions that a "reject from Gotham"
gassed the Daily Planet, killing 48 people.

00:05:24.509 --> 00:05:29.699
That's a direct reference to Mark Waid and
Alex Ross' graphic novel Kingdom Come, on

00:05:29.699 --> 00:05:32.110
which Superman-96 is based.

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:36.899
In that story, the Joker attacks the Daily
Planet and murders Lois Lane, Jimmy Olsen,

00:05:36.899 --> 00:05:39.350
and a bunch of Superman's other friends.

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:44.210
That begins a chain of events that concludes
with Superman hanging up his cape and retiring,

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:48.029
leaving the fate of the world in the hands
of a new generation of heroes.

00:05:48.029 --> 00:05:53.809
Routh's Superman has the same tragic backstory,
he even sports the black-and-red insignia

00:05:53.809 --> 00:05:57.909
that the Kingdom Come Superman wears when
he comes out of hiding, but it would've been

00:05:57.909 --> 00:06:02.749
even more powerful if Crisis had been able
to actually show the Clown Prince of Crime.

00:06:02.749 --> 00:06:07.599
"You wanna know why I use a knife?

00:06:07.599 --> 00:06:09.289
Guns are too quick.

00:06:09.289 --> 00:06:14.550
You can't savor all the little emotions."

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:18.990
Maybe Warner Bros. didn't want to distract
from Joaquin Phoenix's award-winning turn

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:20.229
as the character.

00:06:20.229 --> 00:06:23.099
Maybe the Arrowverse has plans for Joker down
the line.

00:06:23.099 --> 00:06:24.149
Who knows?

00:06:24.149 --> 00:06:29.069
Still, something, even if it was just Batman:
The Animated Series actor Mark Hamill performing

00:06:29.069 --> 00:06:34.099
the Joker's signature laugh offscreen, would've
gone a long way.

